Runs headless-nvim integration tests against the real plugin at `nvim/lua/steno-ts/init.lua` to catch runtime bugs invisible to Rust-side tests — buffer edits, cursor arithmetic, and `vim.snippet.expand` only manifest in actual Neovim. Each test spawns a throwaway `nvim --headless` process, injects Lua that loads the plugin, drives it through `M._try_expand` or `M._token_before_cursor`, and asserts on buffer state.

Requires `nvim >=0.10` on `PATH` (for `vim.snippet`). A missing binary is a loud assertion failure, not a silent skip — mapping tool absence to test pass is exactly what let a wrong assertion here go unnoticed before this check existed.

`require_nvim()` asserts `nvim --version` succeeds; each test calls it first. `run_lua(lua)` writes the script to a temp file with `runtimepath` prepended to `nvim/` so `require("steno-ts")` loads the checked-in plugin, spawns `nvim --headless -u NONE -l`, and returns the `Output`. `assert_lua_ok(lua)` runs the script and turns nonzero exit (an `assert()` failure surfaces on stderr) into a descriptive `Err`.

Tests encode snippet bodies inline in `@@…@@` tokens per ADR-2, using U+2424 (INLINE_NEWLINE) to represent body newlines. `terminal_snippet_places_exit_tabstop_before_closing_brace` verifies that a body-break before the exit tabstop renders to exactly one `\n`, matching `render::tokenize` and `snippet::render_terminal`'s shared `Chunk::BodyBreak` handling. `extending_a_chord_does_not_eat_preceding_text` mirrors Plover's correction model — when a shorter chord extends to a terminal, Plover backspaes what it typed and retyped the token — and asserts that pre-existing text survives the correction.